I wouldn't do a search here, I'd just compare in reverse with the extension, and then check that there's a period ahead of that, that would process the string exactly once, and the result would only be valid if the end of the string is, ignoring case, equal to `"." + p_ext`, so I'd say:
```suggestion
	if (p_ext.length() >= length()) {
		return false;
	}
	
	const char32_t *this_str = get_data() + length() - p_ext.length();
	
	if (*this_char != '.') {
		return false;
	}
	
	this_str++;
	const char32_t *that_str = p_ext.get_data();

	while (true) {
		if (*that_str == 0 && *this_str == 0) {
			return true;
		} else if (*this_str == 0 || that_str == 0) {
			return false;
		} else if (_find_upper(*this_str) < _find_upper(*that_str)) {
			return false;
		} else if (_find_upper(*this_str) > _find_upper(*that_str)) {
			return false;
		}
		this_str++;
		that_str++;
	}

	return false;
```
Or equivalent, haven't tested and might be off on some of the size checks

This *should* check that at one position before the length of `p_ext` there is a `.`, and then traverse over the rest of the string and match the extension fully, it will fail if:
* The string being checked for an extension is shorter than or equal in length to the extension
* It doesn't have a period ahead of the extension (checked early based on just skipping)
* That the extension matches after that

Find isn't relevant as there's only exactly one place where the period can be for this check to be valid

